+## Current activation (44436578418, 2026-11-19): corrected momentum signal and cost sensitivity
+
+A pre-cost audit found a blocking correctness defect in the existing momentum
+evidence. `research/backtest/regime_stability.py::momentum_signals` and
+`mean_reversion_signals` computed `mean(log(closes[i-lookback+1:i+1]))`, which
+is the mean log price level, not a return. For the collected positive price
+series this is always positive, so the "momentum" signal was always long and
+the "reversal" signal always short, independent of lookback. The prior
+momentum SUPPORTED result and the reversal FALSIFIED result were therefore
+about an always-long / always-short position, not about momentum or reversal.
+
+The signal formula was corrected to `sign(log(close[i] / close[i-lookback]))`
+in both functions, four signal-contract unit tests were added, and the
+affected momentum verifiers/checks were updated to the same corrected formula.
+The momentum and lookback-sweep artifacts were regenerated.
+
+Corrected zero-cost results (lookback 5, 10-asset universe): 3
+REGIME_STABLE, 6 CONSISTENT_WITH_NOISE, 1 REGIME_DEPENDENT. No asset has a
+uniformly positive segment-median REGIME_STABLE edge at any lookback; the
+lookback-robustness count is ROBUST=0/10, NO_EDGE=10/10. The corrected
+zero-cost result is therefore not a positive momentum edge.
+
+Predeclared transaction-cost grid: zero; realistic (commission_per_trade=2.0,
+commission_per_share=0.003, slippage_cents=2.0, slippage_proportional=0.0005);
+conservative (exactly 2x every realistic component). All 10 assets x lookbacks
